harnessd: a child is a session, so it gets the idle plan-check — the clock lives on the thread that owns the harness

The operator's ruling, verbatim: *"well childs being just a session should get nags"*.
On `main` a child got **no nag at all** — not a weak one, an absent one. The
`agent/child-todos` merge had just made a parent's `todo_write target=` land on its
child's board, in the child's store row and on the child's pane, and left those rows
*unaskable*: `Sessions::rearm_todo_nag` reads `Sessions::open`, a child is **adopted
into the registry** rather than opened, so the idle clock was never armed for one and
a parent's row sat there until somebody happened to type into the child. That is now
load-bearing rather than tidy: the merge-queue design drives a subagent to completion
*"by nagging them via messages"*, and the nag is the driving half of that sentence.

**Three halves, and the one thing not done.** **ARM** where the rows are visible —
`ParentTodos::upsert_child` already persisted and published; it now also calls
`hub.wake_its_own_reader()`, because a publish is not a wake (`Hub::publish` ends in
`cv.notify_all()`, the parked reader's predicate finds no command and no wake, and it
sleeps on with a new row and no clock armed). **DELIVER** by waking the thread that
owns the child's harness — `serve_child` is that thread, so the clock is held *there*
and `Hub::take_own_work_until(deadline)` gained `OwnWork::TimedOut`, the timed sibling
of the wake that landed tonight in `2c15158`. **A worker never drives a child's turn**:
co-locating the clock with the only executor is what makes that impossible rather than
merely forbidden, and what it costs the worker's wait is nothing — a child's deadline
never enters `Sessions::next_nag_at`, and a child with nothing armed passes `None` and
keeps the old indefinite wait. **One policy, not two copies**: `TodoNagClock` holds
only *when* and *what was last said* (schedule); what may be asked about is still
`nag_should_arm` / `the_check_may_ask_about`, the window is still `TODO_NAG_AFTER`, and
the text is still `Harness::nag_notice`, so a plan whose only open row is postponed is
silent on a child for exactly the reason it is silent on a root.

**The merge with `main` left one door with no clock at all, and it was not the one the
WIP was written against.** `serve_child`'s `ChildCommand::Hear` arm — a parent's
`task_message` between turns starting the child's turn — arrived from `2c15158`, after
this branch was written, so the auto-merge produced an arm that runs a turn and touches
neither half of the clock. A message is a speaker and its turn is a turn: it now calls
`nag.prompt_arrived()` (the parent may have said a new plan, so the once-per-saying must
not outlive the saying) and `rearm_the_idle_check(..., true)`, exactly as the prompt arm
beside it does. Without that, a child told something whose turn then moved its own board
would not be re-armed until some *later* wake happened to notice the version.

**MEASURED, and it changed the test: one parent write cannot prove the wake.** The
preserved WIP's `child_nag.rs` passed. It passed with `hub.wake_its_own_reader()`
deleted from `ParentTodos::upsert_child` — because the fixture spawns the child's
serving thread and the write follows it, so the write usually beats the thread to its
park and `serve_child_under`'s own entry re-arm answers the test. With a 500 ms park
first, the same one-write test failed exactly as it should. So the test now writes
**twice**: the first row is the ruling (a parent's row nags a parked child, with nobody
typing into it), and the second write — issued after the first check has been seen, when
the entry arm is spent and the reader is provably back in `take_own_work_until` — is the
one that can only be answered by the parent's wake. Verified both ways: green as
committed, and red with the wake removed (one `[todo check]` on the child's log instead
of two, the second `wait_for` timing out).

**And one read order in the WIP was a silently lost nag.** `serve_child_under` read the
rows and *then* the board version; a parent's write landing between the two armed the
clock from the OLD rows and then found the version already moved, so the wake re-armed
nothing and the nag was gone for good. The version is the wider read and now goes first:
version-then-rows arms from the old rows, sees the move, and re-arms from the rows the
write just left. Same two facts, no window.

**And the WIP's `due_now` was a nag that quietly went away — found by the WIP's OWN unit
test, which had never been run.** It read `self.due.take()`, so the deadline was spent
*whatever the answer*: a session armed for `t+60`, asked at `t+1` because some OTHER
session's nag (or the sweep) came due, answered "not yet" — and lost its clock, because
`next_nag_at` no longer had a deadline to hand the worker and nothing ever asked again.
The old `HashMap` had this right by filtering on `at <= now` *before* `nag_due.remove`;
that order is now kept where the state lives, and a session that is not due keeps its
deadline. `the_boundary_is_due_and_a_delivery_disarms_either_way` fails on the `take`
and passes on the fix — which is the whole argument for the WIP's three clock unit tests
existing at all.

**Also fixed in the WIP's own source, and in the shape of the tree rather than by
hand.** `child_todos.rs:104` discarded `Option::insert`'s return — the row it replaced
dropped on the floor, and a `must_use` warning nobody had read. And the delivery's
failure path publishes `todo_check_failed`, which the tree's own census refuses to let
exist unclassified: it has a `Class::Failure` row beside `wake_failed`, for the same
argument — the session is not stuck, but a sentence the model was owed did not reach it
— and `warning::the_register_census` moves with it to 32 routine / 9 refused / 49
failures, 90 codes.

**`child_nag.rs` is 505 lines that had never compiled once, and the compile errors were
all in the test** — a claim about how much of the design was ever checked rather than
merely written. Fixed by writing toward the tree's real API rather than the sketch's:
the trait is `letibot_tools::builtins::todo::ChildTodos` (there is no inherent
`ParentTodos::upsert_child`), `std::env::temp_dir`, `fetch_add(1, Ordering)`,
`canned.endpoint.clone()` (`Canned` implements `Drop`, so no field of it can be moved
out), and the serving thread gets its own copy of the child's id because it outlives the
frame that named it.

What the test proves end to end: a real `Harness` opened the way
`HarnessTaskRunner::run_to_completion` opens one, its first turn run, then parked in
`serve_child_under` on its own thread; the parent's write through the real
`ParentTodos`; the child's OWN log holding `[todo check]` as `Speaker::Agent`, naming
the parent by id; once per plan and not on a metronome; and the second write earning a
second check because the plan moved.

    cargo test -p letibot-harnessd --test child_nag
    test result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out
